INT. DECOMMISSIONED PLANETARIUM - NIGHT

A round chamber of tiered seats under a cracked dome. A projector in the centre, shrouded in a tarp. Rain falls through a hole in the ceiling onto the floor and runs toward a drain.

VERA KOSTOPOULOS, forties, in a black raincoat, stands at the foot of the aisle with a flashlight. She sweeps it across the seats.

VERA
Anyone here?

Nothing. She takes a step. Her heel clicks on tile.

A MAN steps out from behind the projector, hands visible. Thirties, drenched, a satchel across his chest.

MAN
You're late.

VERA
You didn't say a time.

MAN
I said midnight.

VERA
It's midnight.

MAN
It's twelve-oh-four.

She lowers the flashlight. He doesn't relax.

VERA
You have it?

MAN
You have the money?

VERA
I have a favour.

MAN
I don't take favours.

VERA
(sighing)
What's your name?

MAN
You don't need my name.

VERA
I need something to call you.

He glances at the dome, at the rain, at the dark gaps between seats.

MAN
Arjun.

VERA
Arjun.

ARJUN
Don't say it twice. It makes it real.

She nods. He opens the satchel, pulls out a thick manila envelope, and holds it up like a flag. She reaches. He pulls it back.

ARJUN (CONT'D)
First tell me who sent you.

VERA
You know who.

ARJUN
Say it.

VERA
Hale.

ARJUN
(muttering)
Hale. God.

A SOUND from the upper tier: a scrape. Both of them go still. Vera lifts the flashlight. The beam finds a seat, empty. Another. Empty. Then a figure on the highest row, sitting calmly with a pistol on his lap.

HALE, fifties, in a camel coat, soft-spoken, says nothing.

ARJUN (CONT'D)
(to Vera)
You brought him.

VERA
I didn't.

HALE
(calling down)
She didn't.

He stands. Descends one step at a time, the gun held loosely, the way a man holds a cup of tea.

HALE (CONT'D)
(to the room)
I do apologise for the theatre. I always did love a planetarium.

ARJUN
Vera, run.

VERA
There's nowhere to run.

HALE
Quite right.

He reaches the aisle. Arjun clutches the satchel. Vera sets down the flashlight, slowly, and the beam rolls across the floor and lights the three of them from below.

HALE (CONT'D)
(gently)
The envelope, Arjun.

Arjun hesitates. Then tosses it. It slaps on the tiles at Hale's feet. He picks it up. Opens it. Looks inside.

It is empty.

HALE (CONT'D)
(still gentle)
Oh, dear.

ARJUN
(small)
I burned it.

HALE
When?

ARJUN
Tonight. Before I came.

Hale laughs, softly, and aims the gun at Arjun's chest. Vera moves, fast, and slams the flashlight into his wrist. The shot cracks off the dome. Rain hisses on the floor.

Vera and Hale grapple. The gun skitters under a seat. Arjun scrambles after it.

VERA
Arjun! Leave it!

ARJUN
I've got it!

HALE
Don't.

Arjun rises, gun in both hands, shaking. He aims at Hale. Hale raises his palms. The rain falls between them.

ARJUN
(to himself)
Breathe. Just breathe.

VERA
Arjun. Look at me. Put it down.

He lowers it an inch. Another. The dome groans. A chunk of plaster falls and shatters beside the projector, and when they all look up, Hale is gone.

VERA (CONT'D)
(to the empty room)
Hale!

Only the rain answers.
